<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles the Child</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 847–<a href="/years/866">866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Charles of Aquitaine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles the Child was a son of the West Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>, who made him king of Aquitaine in 855 to win the Aquitanian nobility away from the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pippin-of-herstal">Pippin II</a>. His rule was contested and largely nominal, and he rebelled against his father in the early 860s. He died in 866 from the effects of a head wound received in a quarrel, and Aquitaine passed to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-stammerer">Louis the Stammerer</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-the-child.jpg" alt="Charles the Child" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denier_-_Charles_the_Bald_-_Melle_Mint.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
